course: everyday
lesson: 464
title: Growing apart
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In Year 3 Sam and Jake did everything together: dens, conkers, the same rubbish joke about the dinner lady's gravy. In Year 4 Jake starts hanging round the Year 6 lads by the bike shed, who call Sam «the little nerd». Now when Sam walks over, Jake looks at his shoes. Sam stops walking over. He finds two mates at chess club. Once, when nobody's looking, Jake does their old secret handshake in the corridor. Sam's dad tells him about Gaz-not-Big-Gaz, his best mate at school: «We swore blood brothers. Then he got into rugby and I got into computers. No fight, son. Just life. Haven't spoken in twenty years.»
explain: Lily and Chloe used to spend every Saturday making slime. Now Chloe watches make-up videos and says slime is «so Year 2». Lily is still dinosaur-mad. Their last sleepover ends with both of them on their phones in total silence. Nobody did anything wrong, and it still leaves a strange empty bit inside. Lily later finds a girl at museum club who can name every sauropod (the long-necked dinosaurs). Mia's best friend Ava starts going round with a crowd who say Mia's pony stickers are «cringe» and «giving baby» — Gen Alpha talk for «looks babyish». A week later Ava says it too, loudly, in front of them.
explain: Mia keeps her stickers. At riding lessons she meets Kenzie, who has a pony-shaped pencil case and zero shame, and by spring they're inseparable. Chloe sees a photo online of her old best friend Aisha at a party with girls Chloe doesn't know, all in matching sunglasses; she wasn't even told. «It's alright to feel left out, love,» says her mum. «Text her something nice.» Chloe sends «cute glasses lol»; Aisha sends back three hearts. And when Jake and Sam bump into each other at the pool, there's an awkward silence, then: «Race you, loser buys the Freddos.» They race like Year 3 again, and Sam wins. Drifting can be fixed, too, when there's a real problem to talk about. Maddie tells Lily, «You never want to do anything fun any more.» Lily snaps back, «You never listen, you just boss.» They stomp home opposite ways. A week later, on the swings, they talk properly and make a deal: one of Maddie's mad ideas, then one of Lily's quiet ones, every weekend.
explain: Connor's nursery friend Ben still wants «dragons and knights» every break, with sound effects. Connor is nine and feels about forty. «Bruh, I'm not doing the roar again.» Instead of ditching Ben, he says, «Come on, let's do footy,» and Ben turns out to be a beast in goal. Kieran's fourteen-year-old brother says, «You'll have different mates every year till secondary, bro.» Kieran says no chance — him and Tom are for life. By Year 5, Tom is one of two mates he's swapped, though they still say hi in the corridor. Clearing his drawer, Kieran finds a photo of him and Tom in Year 2, gap-toothed, holding a frog. «Dead weird,» he says. His mum says, «You'll remember the good bits. That's what they're for.»
fact: Friends sometimes grow apart because they start liking different things, and often nobody is to blame.
fact: Drifting apart can hurt even when there has been no fight.
fact: Friendships at primary school change a lot as children grow, and that is normal, not a failure.
fact: Begging a drifting friend or sulking rarely helps; finding people who share what you love now usually does.
fact: You don't have to give up what you love to keep a friend; the right friends like you as you are.
fact: When a friendship feels wrong because of a real problem, talking about it calmly can fix it, while silence lets it drift.
fact: You can grow apart kindly, by suggesting new things together instead of dropping the person.
fact: Seeing photos of an old friend having fun without you can sting; reaching out or letting go both work better than staring at the photos.
fact: Drifted friends can still have good moments together and be friendly.
fact: Old friendships still count and are worth remembering, even when they are over.
fact: Grown-ups often lose touch with school friends too, without any fight.
fact: «Cringe» is slang for embarrassing, «giving baby» means «looks babyish», «bruh» is like «mate» said with a sigh, and a «beast» at something is really good at it.
exercise: Why did Jake look at his shoes when Sam walked over? | He'd moved to a crowd that didn't want Sam | Sam had been mean to him the day before at football | He'd lost his football | He was looking for conkers
exercise: What did Sam do once Jake drifted? | Stopped begging and found mates at chess club | Followed Jake everywhere he went at break | Told the teacher Jake was mean | Joined the Year 6 lads
exercise: What did Sam's dad's story about Gaz show? | Drifting apart happens to everyone, with no fight | Dad and Gaz had a huge argument and never made up | Rugby players can't use computers | Blood brothers never drift apart
exercise: Why did Lily and Chloe drift apart? | They grew into liking different things | Lily stole Chloe's slime and wouldn't give it back | Chloe moved to another country | Their mums fell out
exercise: Ava's new crowd called Mia's stickers «giving baby». What does that mean? | They look babyish | They're for a new baby | They're very expensive | They are brilliant
exercise: What did Mia do about her pony stickers? | Kept them and found a friend who liked ponies too | Threw them all away so Ava's crowd would like her | Gave them to Ava's crowd | Hid them under her bed forever
exercise: What does Mia's story teach? | Right friends like you as you are | Always change to fit the crowd | Pony stickers are only for babies | Old friends always come back
exercise: Why did Maddie and Lily stomp off in opposite directions? | Each felt the other didn't listen or join in | Lily broke Maddie's swing | Maddie was moving away and hadn't told Lily | They both wanted the same seat
exercise: How did Maddie and Lily fix their friendship? | Talked calmly and took turns choosing | Stopped speaking for a year | Asked a teacher to choose every game for them | Let Maddie decide everything
exercise: How did Connor grow apart from Ben kindly? | He suggested footy instead of dragons | He told Ben he was babyish | He ran away every break | He kept on playing the dragons game forever
exercise: What did Connor find out about Ben in goal? | Ben was brilliant at it | Ben was scared of the ball | Ben only plays as a dragon | Ben refused to play at all
exercise: Kieran said he and Tom were friends for life. What happened? | They drifted, though they still say hi | They became even closer than before | They had a terrible fight in the playground | Tom moved to the moon
exercise: Was Kieran's brother right about friends changing? | Mostly yes, it's normal as you grow | No, real friends never change at all | Only for brothers and sisters | Only after you start secondary school
exercise: Chloe sees Aisha's party photo she wasn't told about. What helps more than staring at it? | Reaching out or letting go | Posting a mean comment under it | Copying the sunglasses she wore | Staring until she feels better
exercise: Jake and Sam race at the pool. What does that show? | Drifted friends can still have good moments | They were best friends again, forever and ever | They hate each other | Racing ends friendships
exercise: What did Kieran's mum say about the old photo? | You keep the good memories | Throw it away, it's over | Ring Tom right now | Old friends don't matter
test: «sam must've done summat wrong, that's why jake ditched him». What's more likely? | They grew into different things, no one's crime | Sam was secretly horrible to Jake when no one looked | Jake was paid to leave | Sam stole Jake's conkers
test: Ava: «ur stickers are so cringe». Mia's options — which is wisest? | Keep what she loves and find people who share it | Bin the stickers and buy cooler ones, so Ava stays | Copy whatever Ava's crowd likes | Never speak to anyone again
test: Lily and Maddie are snapping at each other every weekend. What would most likely happen if they just stayed silent? | They'd drift further apart | It would fix itself by Friday | They'd become closer than ever | Someone else would fix it for them
test: Ben wants to play dragons again. Connor: «bruh, no». Which move keeps it kind? | Suggest something new to play together | Play dragons every day till Ben gets bored of it | Hide in the toilets every break | Tell everyone Ben is a baby
test: «friends who drift apart were never real friends anyway». True? | No, real friendships can end without anyone failing | Yes, real friends never change, so they must have been fakes | Yes, that's how you spot fakes | No, they were enemies all along
test: Chloe keeps scrolling Aisha's party pics and feels worse. Her mum's tip? | Text something nice and see what happens | Delete all her own photos | Make a better party next door | Comment «who invited YOU» on every photo
test: In a film the two best friends drift apart, then one has to save the other from a burning building to be friends again. What's more real? | A small chat or a game can bring back a good moment | Only a big disaster or a rescue can fix a friendship | Drifted friends can never speak again | You need a fire to stay friends
test: Kieran: «my brother's chatting rubbish, me and Tom are forever». What's true by Year 5? | Friendships often change, and that's normal | Brothers always lie about friends | Kieran and Tom became twins | Kieran never makes new friends
test: What would have happened if Sam kept following Jake to the bike shed? | More «little nerd» and more hurt | Jake would have dropped his new mates | The Year 6 lads would have made Sam king | Nothing at all would change for Sam
test: Gaz-not-Big-Gaz and Sam's dad haven't spoken in twenty years. Was there a fight? | No, they just grew in different directions | Yes, a huge one about rugby that nobody forgot | Yes, over a computer | Nobody knows, it was a secret
test: «Me and Tom are basically strangers now». Is the Year 2 frog photo worthless now? | No, old friendships still count | Yes, bin it | Yes, unless Tom wants it | No, it's worth money
exam: Identify the principal cause of the gradual separation of two friends who have quarrelled with neither. | The development of differing interests as they mature | The secret malice of one party towards the other party | The intervention of their teachers | The dishonesty of both parties
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the abandonment of a cherished pastime in order to retain a friend? | It is unnecessary, since suitable friends accept one as one is | It is the sole means of retaining any friendship | It is required by most schools | It invariably restores the friendship
exam: Given that two friends quarrel repeatedly over the choice of activities, ascertain the course most likely to preserve their friendship. | A calm discussion and an agreement to alternate choices | Continued silence on both sides until the matter resolves itself | The permanent surrender of choice by one party | The referral of every choice to a teacher
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the value of a friendship that has ended without quarrel. | Its memories retain worth after its conclusion | It is shown never to have been genuine | It ought to be forgotten entirely | It obliges both parties to resume it
